1. What “Binding” Means (Simple Definition)

Binding = cryptographically tying identity + request + payload together so nothing can be swapped, replayed, or reused.

In your system, you bind:

Session (who)
+ Request (which)
+ Nonce (when/one-time)
+ Payload (what)
🔗 2. What Exactly Is Your Binding?

You use JWT (bindingToken):

{
  "sid": "sessionId",
  "rid": "requestId",
  "nonce": "xyz",
  "exp": 1710000000
}

👉 Signed by server (private key)

Then client signs:

SHA-256(payload)
+ nonce
+ bindingToken (JWT)
+ requestId
+ ephemeralPublicKey
🔐 3. Trust Chain (This is your key explanation)

Explain this to architect:

Server signs JWT  → proves identity + request context
Client verifies JWT → trusts context
Client signs payload + JWT → binds payload to that context
Server verifies everything → confirms full integrity

👉 This is called:

“End-to-end cryptographic binding”

🧩 4. Client-Side Validation (Step-by-step)
Step 1: Receive bindingToken (JWT)

Client gets:

bindingToken
Step 2: Verify JWT signature
verify(bindingToken, serverPublicKey)

👉 Ensures:

JWT is issued by your server
Not tampered
Step 3: Validate claims (light validation)

Client checks:

exp not expired
rid matches requestId
nonce matches

👉 Prevents:

stale or injected tokens
Step 4: Use JWT in signing
Sign(
  SHA-256(payload) +
  nonce +
  bindingToken +
  requestId +
  ephemeralPublicKey
)

👉 This step is CRITICAL

🔥 What this achieves (client side)

Client guarantees:

“I am signing THIS payload for THIS request issued by server”

🧩 5. Server-Side Validation (Step-by-step)

Now server receives encrypted data.

Step 1: Decrypt everything

Extract:

payload
signature
ephemeralPublicKey
bindingToken
Step 2: Verify JWT
verify(bindingToken, serverPublicKey)

Then validate claims:

sid == sessionId (from request/session)
rid == requestId (DB)
nonce == stored nonce
exp not expired

👉 This ensures:

JWT belongs to this request
JWT is valid and fresh
Step 3: Validate DB state
status == INIT
nonce not used

👉 Prevents replay

Step 4: Verify signature

Recompute:

SHA-256(payload)

Then verify:

verify signature using ephemeralPublicKey

Ensure signed data includes:

SHA-256(payload)
+ nonce
+ bindingToken
+ requestId
+ ephemeralPublicKey
🔥 What this proves (server side)

Server now knows:

✔ Payload not changed
✔ Request not changed
✔ JWT not changed
✔ Bound to correct session
✔ Signed by client (fresh key)
✔ Not replayed
✔ User passed MFA
🎯 6. Why Include JWT in Signature?

This is the most important interview question

❌ If you DON’T include JWT

Attacker can:

Take valid signature
Replace JWT with another session
Replay request

👉 Identity swap attack

✅ Because you DO include JWT
Sign(... + bindingToken + ...)

👉 If JWT changes → signature breaks

👉 So:

JWT is cryptographically locked to the payload

🔄 7. Full Binding Equation (Explain This)

Tell architect:

Signature binds:

Payload
+ Nonce
+ RequestId
+ JWT (session binding)
+ Ephemeral Key
🧠 8. What Attacks Are Prevented
🚫 Replay attack
nonce + status + exp
🚫 Payload tampering
SHA-256 + signature
🚫 Session hijacking
JWT binding
🚫 Request swapping
requestId inside JWT + signature
🚫 Key substitution
ephemeralPublicKey inside signature
🚫 Man-in-the-middle
encryption + signature
💡 9. Simple Analogy (Use This in Presentation)

“Server gives a signed token saying:
‘This request belongs to Puneet, session XYZ’

Client signs payload along with that token

Server verifies both its own token and client signature

So nothing can be changed or reused.”

🏁 10. Final One-Line Explanation

If architect asks:

👉 “How does binding work?”

Say:

“We issue a server-signed JWT that binds session, request, and nonce.
The client verifies it and includes it in the signature.
This ensures the payload, request, and identity are cryptographically tied and cannot be altered or replayed.”

🔥 Bonus (If they go deeper)

If they ask:
👉 “Why not just JWT?”

Answer:

“JWT ensures identity, but not payload integrity.
Signature ensures payload integrity.
Combining both gives full binding.”
